es not change the answer's correctness. Return only a JSON object with unsupported_probability and source_sentence_id. Use null when no visible source sentence is a proposed witness.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n{\"complete_source\": \"2  Also, Three-toed sloths have a small tail and its forelegs are substantially longer than the rear ones.ifference Between Two and Three Toed Sloths: 1  Besides the fact that one has two toes on its forelimbs and the other three, they have different numbers of vertebrae (three-toed ones have nine; two-toed ones have six or seven).\\\\n\\\\n\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"what makes the two toed sloth different\\\"} 4  Three claws on hind limbs.\\\\n\\\\npassage 3:1 Besides the fact that one has two toes on its forelimbs and the other three, they have different numbers of vertebrae (three-toed ones have nine; two-toed ones have six or seven). 3  Three-toed sloths have three fingers on their hands. 2  Two-toed sloths have hands with two functioning fingers with sharp claws 76 - 102 mm (3 - 4 inches). Long limbs — forelegs only slightly longer than hind legs (3-toed sloth's forelegs are 35% longer than hind legs). 3  2-toed is larger, faster, and nocturnal. 2  They are now placed in separate families. They are about the size of a medium-sized dog at around 23 to 27 inches (58 to 68 cm) and 17.5 to 18.75 pounds (about 8 kilograms).\\\\n\\\\npassage 2:1 Two-toed and three-toed sloths were formerly placed in the same family but the two genera have profound behavioral and anatomical differences and are believed to come from two different fossil lineages. They are about the size of a medium-sized dog at around 23 to 27 inches (58 to 68 cm) and 17.5 to 18.75 pounds (about 8 kilograms).he two-toed sloth is slightly bigger than the three-toed sloth, though they share many of the same features. {\\\"passages\\\":\\\"passage 1:The two-toed sloth is slightly bigger than the three-toed sloth, though they share many of the same features.\", \"original_answer\": \"Based on the provided passages, the main differences between the two-toed sloth (Choloepus didactylus) and the three-toed sloth (Bradypus variegatus) are:\\n\\n1. Number of toes on the front limbs: The two-toed sloth has two toes on each front limb, while the three-toed sloth has three toes.\\n2. Size: The two-toed sloth is generally smaller than the three-toed sloth, with a length of around 23-27 inches (58-68 cm) and a weight of around 17.5-18.75 pounds (about 8 kilograms), compared to the three-toed sloth's length of around 30-40 inches (76-102 cm) and weight of around 20-30 pounds (around 9-14 kilograms).\\n3. Limb structure: The two-toed sloth has longer limbs overall, with the forelegs being slightly longer than the hind legs, while the three-toed sloth has shorter forelegs that are 35% longer than the hind legs.\\n4. Hand structure: The two-toed sloth has hands with two functioning fingers and sharp claws, while the three-toed sloth has three fingers on its hands.\\n5. Vertebral count: The two-toed sloth has six or seven vertebrae in its body, while the three-toed sloth has nine vertebrae.\\n6. Tail length: The three-toed sloth has a smaller tail than the two-toed sloth.\\n7. Forelimb length: The three-toed sloth has substantially longer forelegs than the rear legs, while the two-toed sloth has more balanced proportions.\", \"target\": \"any_unsupported_span_in_original_response\", \"visible_source_sentence_ids\": [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], \"source_evidence_erased\": false}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","generated":""}
