na told NBC's Lester Holt on Wednesday that he saw the two men struggling on the ground. In two interviews with NBC, Santana said that he was walking to his job in North Charleston on Saturday morning when he saw Slager chase Scott, who had been pulled over for a broken taillight. His account contradicts what Slager told dispatchers. Feidin Santana, the witness who recorded the video, said he didn't see Scott grab Slager's Taser. Witness: I nearly erased shooting video out of fear. Slager is seen in the video handcuffing Scott after the shooting. Later in the video, when Slager approaches Scott's body, Slager appears to drop a dark object near Scott. It's unclear whether that is the Taser. Before Slager opens fire, the video shows a dark object falling behind Scott and hitting the ground. The officer initially said that he used a Taser on Scott, who, Slager said, tried to take the weapon. It's unclear what happened before Scott ran, or why he ran. If words were exchanged between the men, they're are not audible on the tape. Scott was hit five times. A bystander's cell phone video, which began after an alleged struggle on the ground between Slager and Scott, shows the five-year police veteran shooting at Scott eight times as Scott runs away. Slager has been fired and charged with murder in the death of 50-year-old Walter Scott.\\nEyewitness video showing white North Charleston police Officer Michael Slager shooting to death an unarmed black man has exposed discrepancies in the reports of the first officers on the scene.\", \"original_answer\": \"South Carolina police officer Michael Slager has been charged with murder after shooting an unarmed black man, Walter Scott, eight times. A video of the incident, taken by passerby Feidin Santana, shows that Slager shot at Scott as he ran away, contrary to the initial police report which stated that Scott had tried to take Slager's Taser. The video shows a dark object falling behind Scott and hitting the ground before Slager 